readings.c2s3:
¶
'''buffalo-berry''' (buf′a̤-lō-ber″i), ''n.'' '''1.''' The fruit of the ''Shepherdia argentea'', a shrub or small tree which grows in western North America.—'''2.''' The tree itself.

'''buffalo-bird''' (buf′a̤-lō-bėrd), ''n.'' A bird of the genus ''Sturnopastor'': so called because it associates with buffaloes.
{{smaller block|I never tired of watching the friendly relation between the ''Buffalo-birds'' (Sturnopastor ialla and S. melanopterus) and their bovine hosts.
{{right|''H. O. Forbes'', Eastern Archipelago, p. 55.}}}}

'''buffalo-bug''' (buf′a̤-lō-bug), ''n.'' A name of the carpet-beetle.

'''buffalo-chips''' (buf′a̤-lō-chips), ''n. pl.'' The dry dung of the bison, formerly used for fuel on the western plains of North America.

'''buffalo-cod''' (buf′a̤-lō-kod), ''n.'' A chiroid fish, ''Ophiodon elongatus''; the cultus-cod.

'''buffalo-fish''' (buf′a̤-lō-fish), ''n.'' The popular name of fishes of the family ''Catostomidæ'', or suckers, and genus ''Ictiobus'' or ''Bubalichthys''. {{smaller|They are among the largest of the suckers, somewhat resemble carp, and abound in the lakes and rivers of the United States. The name was probably given on account of the protuberant or hump-like back, which rises highest near the front of the dorsal fin. Several species are recognized. See ''Ictiobinæ''.}}

'''buffalo-gnat''' (buf′a̤-lō-nat), ''n.'' A kind of black-fly, a dipterous insect of the genus ''Simulium'' and family ''Simuliidæ''. {{smaller|It is found in almost incredible numbers in the southern and western United States, and is a dreaded pest of cattle, rendering the animals frantic, and in some cases causing death.}}

